A||N||12||87ECO5||Insurance companies that insure for appraised values||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||01-26-2001||10:02 AM||||I ran a search to find answers to this question but, there's so much stuff on here related to insurance, it was hard to filter through.  I'm sure this has been discussed already so, if you know the thread(s), please direct me to it (them).<P>I'm about to do a series of suspension, interior and engine modifications to my '87 coupe that approach $7000.  Unfortunately, my present insurance company says they will NOT insure a car for more than its book value.<P>Can someone offer some suggestions on insurance companies that insure for appraised values - not just book values?  I've already received some good information from Oreif (thank you, sir) but would like more.<P>Suggestions on appraisal companies would be greatly appreciated too.<P>I need to get this done quickly as the car heads down to Ed Parks' place in three weeks for its engine transplant.  I don't want to drive it home without all that work being insured.<P>Thank you in advance ...||||reg||5
Z||000001||ChadMan||01-26-2001||10:17 AM||||Progressive will do this.  My agent told me if I went ahead with a 3.4 conversion, I could purchase a "custom parts" rider on my car that would pay me an additional $4000 if my car were stolen or I crashed it.<P>Not sure how it would work out if some numbnuts wrecked me and I had to try and collect that extra money from his/her insurance company though!||||reg||14
Z||000002||Old Lar||01-26-2001||10:31 AM||||You can get the car appraised to determine its value, then have it insured for that amount.  "Professional" appraisers may charge for this service.<P>Or if the car is driven just for pleasure, you can also get a break.  And in some states if the car is 15 years old, you can get classic insurance or historical insurance.  Unfortunately in NY the car has to be 25 years old to qualify for historical plates.||||reg||3
Z||000003||Oreif||01-26-2001||10:48 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.autoappraisal.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autoappraisal.com/</A>  <A HREF="http://www.hvaa.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.hvaa.com/</A> <P>Here are 2 places I found On-line. You can look in your local phone book under appraisals. Look by Ed Parks Place and have it done right after the work is done.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||01-26-2001||11:16 AM||||I just called my State Farm agent(I still hate that woman), and you can rule S.F. out. She suggested checking with Progressive, but didn't know of any others. I'm leery of dealing with an online based insurance company(I think they are online based), since I like dealing with people face to face, but I haven't heard many negative thing about them.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ccollins||01-26-2001||11:41 AM||||My insurance company is Farmers Insurance and they told me I could do the same thing.  Get the car appraised for a higher insured amount and also add any special equipment riders that I wanted. <P>Mine is currently stock so haven't had to do that just yet, I don't know how much extra this will cost.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||01-26-2001||11:55 AM||||Ive checked into special insurance for classic and kitcars. One was only $310 a year for value at $20,000. The rules were I had to have another registered car (I do), have to keep it garaged (yes) and have to have some form of security system at garage (I do), unlimited milage. Now the kicker was I could not drive the car except to shows, parades etc. I like driving mine so I told em to stick it. I have State Farm and according to what my agent says, if i keep all my reciepts, and supplied him with pics my car was covered to be repaired as is or value of car plus the receipts, allowing for depreciation. Im just hoping no one ever hits me so I have to test it out. <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||KEV||01-27-2001||10:54 AM||||I HAD FARMERS INS. ON MY 85GT 3800V6 MODIFIED, IT HAD AS THEY CALLED IT PLEASURE USE INS. INCLUDED IS FULL COVERAGE AND IT WAS APPRAISED AT $7,000 AND fARMERS INS. IT FOR THE TOLD AMOUNT. IT ONLY COST ME ABOUT $650 A YEAR. BUT I CHANGED AND WENT TO GRUNDY'S INS. BUT I ONLY USE IT FOR SHOWS AND SUNDAY DRIVES. THEY ONLY CHARGE ME $137 A YEAR FOR THE SAME COVERAGE AND A HIGHER APPRAISED VALUE.||||reg||1
Z||000008||HAGS||01-27-2001||11:22 AM||||lol, all you had to do was look for a show or parade somewhere in the general direction you were headed and you'd be safe  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  "uhhh...yes officer, I was headed to the San Francisco(random) Fiero Show"||||reg||1
Z||000009||HAGS||01-27-2001||11:23 AM||||I dont know how much my insurance is...my dad pays it since we are on the same thing orsomething like that...but my car is worth less than $1000 so it shouldnt be too much...cant wait until I can get a Fiero that costs alot to insure though...<p>[This message has been edited by HAGS (edited 01-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||01-27-2001||12:08 PM||||what they said was I couldnt even drive it to lunch, or on a date, etc. I told them it was strictly for pleasure not business but they said no errands, dates, around town driving. So if I cant enjoy driving it I told them I wasnt interested.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by HAGS:<BR><B>lol, all you had to do was look for a show or parade somewhere in the general direction you were headed and you'd be safe   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  "uhhh...yes officer, I was headed to the San Francisco(random) Fiero Show"</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||87ECO5||01-30-2001||08:10 AM||||I called State Farm (I was with them before I switched to U.S.A.A.).<P>They told me they don't insure a car at an appraised value.  However, they have something called "value at time of loss."  Apparently, if you can produce receipts that show $$$ amounts, they will reimburse those amounts.<P>Why do I feel uncomfortable about that option?<P>Me:  "Hi, I just totaled my car."<BR>SF:  "OK, Mr. Haas, we'll send a check out right away for the book value - which is $1975."<BR>Me:  "Uh, OK but I have receipts showing I put $7500 worth of modifications into the vehicle too."<BR>SF:  "No problem, Mr. Haas.  We'll mail a check out for that too.  Oh, and we'll also be mailing out your cancellation notice!!!!!"||||reg||1
Z||000012||ChadMan||01-30-2001||08:28 AM||||Do NOT trust Scam Farm under ANY circumstances.  Recall my 86 SE story -- I kept it in storage the first six months I had it and didn't get insurance until I brought it home, and they tried to charge me high-risk rates for owning an uninsured vehicle.<P>"Like a gay lover, State Farm is there."||||reg||4
